Cell["\<\
The specification may equally be given as a rule between lists of index \
labels:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"424fe946-46ff-d149-90e5-add1656ae05f"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
{Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{a, b}, \"ij,jk->ik\"],
  Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{a, b}, {{1, 2}, {2, 3}} -> {1, 3}]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"772f416c-11e0-1949-baa8-13db5a757018"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"2", ",", "1"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"4", ",", "3"}], "}"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"2", ",", "1"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"4", ",", "3"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUU\
ID->"2ec08ee0-0c23-8b49-9666-9923dc29df0b"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Repeating an index within one array takes a diagonal, and leaving it out of \
the output sums it, so a trace and a transpose are contractions too:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5046a8dc-d599-c942-b63c-904155f42dbe"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
{Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{a}, \"ii->i\"], Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{a}, \"ii->\"],
  Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{a}, \"ij->ji\"]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"06f0a2b7-64a4-0346-9e63-62e8676a4a59"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "4"}], "}"}], ",", "5", ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"1", ",", "3"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"2", ",", "4"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUU\
ID->"dd028f57-823b-1445-b4c1-7a0a475399cc"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "An index may occur in any number of the arrays at once, and may occur in \
the output as well as in the inputs. That is what lets a single specification \
express the three-way contractions of the ternary products, which no sequence \
of pairwise contractions would give."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"26159738-9a2e-9b4e-9d1c-0c74e619939a"],

Cell["\<\
The Bhattacharya-Mesner product of three 3-arrays joins all three on one \
index:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0c8b72b8-c88d-9f45-a288-0dadc96e07a9"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
x = Array[#1 + #2 + #3 &, {2, 2, 2}];
Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{x, x, x}, \"ijp,ipk,pjk->ijk\"]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"9cb6532b-426f-6949-9e01-6cd551ff43b6"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"91", ",", "148"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"148", ",", "230"}], "}"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"148", ",", "230"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"230", ",", "341"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",Expressi\
onUUID->"2f9c8c97-ca85-6847-be36-ebd103d27355"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "The result comes back as a NumericalArray when its entries lie in a \
recognized value domain, and as a plain nested list when they are arbitrary \
expressions. Contracting every index away leaves a single entry, which is the \
array of order zero: numerical in gives a NumericalArray of order zero, whose \
Normal is the scalar itself. An array of arbitrary symbolic expressions fits \
neither array object: a numerical array wants entries in a domain, and a \
symbolic array is a name together with dimensions. Whether that gap deserves \
a third object is an open question."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"2e586a36-addc-9b4b-bd6c-9a80a1251124"],

Cell["\<\
The other side of the same coin: an array of order zero is named in a \
specification by an empty group of index labels, which is how a scalar enters \
a contraction.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"85788179-f62e-7048-ae11-76d5d1bb7b71"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{3, a}, \",ij->ij\"]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"b1c2a3d2-efbb-c947-9b51-fe40bb7cb277"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"3", ",", "6"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"9", ",", "12"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->\
"b64e5288-1a00-464c-aa52-9926e770a344"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Numerical and symbolic arrays", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"7820887e-4579-7f43-8007-b7a093789a4c"],

Cell["\<\
A symbolic array is a list of lists of terms with explicit indices, and a \
contraction of symbolic arrays simply builds the corresponding expressions in \
a list of lists. The symbols behave as Wolfram Language symbols always do, \
which is to say as complex variables:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4a7d6ac7-3d76-3d4c-9ffa-7e586b1eb44b"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
sa = Normal @ SymbolicArray[p, {2, 2}];
sb = Normal @ SymbolicArray[q, {2, 2}];
{sa, sb}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"75dc3491-4785-434c-9e98-8ff1bbd7095f"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"p", "[", 
        RowBox[{"1", ",", "1"}], "]"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"p", "[", 
        RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"p", "[", 
        RowBox[{"2", ",", "1"}], "]"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"p", "[", 
        RowBox[{"2", ",", "2"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"q", "[", 
        RowBox[{"1", ",", "1"}], "]"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"q", "[", 
        RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"q", "[", 
        RowBox[{"2", ",", "1"}], "]"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"q", "[", 
        RowBox[{"2", ",", "2"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",E\
xpressionUUID->"9616c1e4-9088-7246-a7bd-f5ef114dd60b"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["ArrayMultiply[{sa, sb}, \"ij,jk->ik\"]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"f06f6eaf-0bef-4e4e-a6b4-de14329a69cf"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"p", "[", 
        RowBox[{"1", ",", "1"}], "]"}], " ", 
       RowBox[{"q", "[", 
        RowBox[{"1", ",", "1"}], "]"}]}], "+", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"p", "[", 
        RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "]"}], " ", 
       RowBox[{"q", "[", 
        RowBox[{"2", ",", "1"}], "]"}]}]}], ",", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"p", "[", 
        RowBox[{"1", ",", "1"}], "]"}], " ", 
       RowBox[{"q", "[", 
        RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "]"}]}], "+", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"p", "[", 
        RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "]"}], " ", 
       RowBox[{"q", "[", 
        RowBox[{"2", ",", "2"}], "]"}]}]}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"p", "[", 
        RowBox[{"2", ",", "1"}], "]"}], " ", 
       RowBox[{"q", "[", 
        RowBox[{"1", ",", "1"}], "]"}]}], "+", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"p", "[", 
        RowBox[{"2", ",", "2"}], "]"}], " ", 
       RowBox[{"q", "[", 
        RowBox[{"2", ",", "1"}], "]"}]}]}], ",", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"p", "[", 
        RowBox[{"2", ",", "1"}], "]"}], " ", 
       RowBox[{"q", "[", 
        RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "]"}]}], "+", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"p", "[", 
        RowBox[{"2", ",", "2"}], "]"}], " ", 
       RowBox[{"q", "[", 
        RowBox[{"2", ",", "2"}], "]"}]}]}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",Expres\
sionUUID->"2a05179f-1266-e049-afa6-a954536ff1ae"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["It agrees with the built-in matrix product, as it should:", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"799052a7-d3bc-484d-b9df-cf1c1912c4f0"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["ArrayMultiply[{sa, sb}, \"ij,jk->ik\"] === sa . sb", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"740f1b19-d463-0a4c-a08a-704d35c58b6e"],

Cell[BoxData["True"], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"481259a1-9eda-6141-b0c2-b35ffac3e4b8"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "A contraction of numerical arrays gives back a NumericalArray; a \
contraction of symbolic ones gives the plain list of lists it builds. The two \
kinds may not be mixed in one call. A contraction of one numerical and one \
symbolic array would be neither, and its entries would have no consistent \
reading, so it is refused."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"16ceadec-2cc5-b945-bbb5-41e599183e6f"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Quiet @ ArrayMultiply[{{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}, sb}, \"ij,jk->ik\"]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"6f7733b7-2fab-d14d-bdcd-bd148dcdfdcc"],

Cell[BoxData["$Failed"], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"510b3215-4333-684d-975f-19f55208ead2"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Contracting every index: the trace", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"bbdfa94b-4131-aa4d-b708-82f25a399c23"],

Cell["\<\
An operation need not return an array. Contracting a single matrix onto no \
indices at all sums its diagonal, which is the trace, and works on numerical \
and symbolic entries alike:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7b81a93f-cc1c-944e-a1fb-524a5ceba275"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
trace[u_List] := Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{u}, \"pp->\"];
{trace[{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}], trace[sa]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"9b9293cf-9572-df40-b799-c39fe5be9088"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"5", ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"p", "[", 
     RowBox[{"1", ",", "1"}], "]"}], "+", 
    RowBox[{"p", "[", 
     RowBox[{"2", ",", "2"}], "]"}]}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->\
"b98964ab-1f28-a94a-955a-e80e8e6022d7"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
{trace[{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}] === Tr[{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}], trace[sa] === Tr[sa]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"124a08b1-e029-3842-94de-6fc86da79abf"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"True", ",", "True"}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"30923e6c-2d43-ed40-8ae5-0dbe48e8ccf0"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "The Normal is doing something here. A contraction of numerical arrays onto \
no indices gives back the 0-array rather than a bare number, and Normal takes \
its single entry. A contraction of symbolic ones gives the expression \
directly, as it gives plain entries in every other case, so the one \
definition covers both."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"95f8187c-2064-4640-bd56-5ddb58c9427a"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["7. Adding and multiplying entry by entry", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"4fb50b35-380e-c149-bb34-d76ab485c293"],

Cell["\<\
Contraction is one of the two things one does with arrays. The other is to \
combine them entry by entry, which is what makes arrays of a given shape a \
vector space rather than merely a collection of tables. ArrayAdd and \
ArrayTimes do that:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"75c02e13-d9ec-6047-b6ae-e77c0e555e62"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
u = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}}; v = {{5, 6}, {7, 8}};
{Normal @ ArrayAdd[u, v], Normal @ ArrayTimes[u, v]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"e3946f55-9349-4b4f-9264-c0d8bb365fb1"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"6", ",", "8"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"10", ",", "12"}], "}"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"5", ",", "12"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"21", ",", "32"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",Expression\
UUID->"cb39c329-cb92-3b4f-b5cf-badd5023cdfa"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Plus and Times on array objects are those same operations, so arrays may be \
combined in ordinary arithmetic notation:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5091e591-77aa-944c-83bd-aa5bcb7c3045"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
{Normal[NumericalArray[u] + NumericalArray[v]],
  Normal[NumericalArray[u] - NumericalArray[v]],
  Normal[NumericalArray[u] NumericalArray[v]]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"7cb5914e-bc11-7849-8cb5-1c83b37d0853"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"6", ",", "8"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"10", ",", "12"}], "}"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"-", "4"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"-", "4"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"-", "4"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"-", "4"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"5", ",", "12"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"21", ",", "32"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",Expression\
UUID->"d3ceefc8-6fa5-834f-90c0-45842d460e53"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
An array of order zero has no shape of its own, so it combines with an array \
of any dimensions, reaching every entry:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"639857ec-6977-844e-80ea-b522f738c48a"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["{Normal @ ArrayTimes[2, u], Normal @ ArrayAdd[1, u]}", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"52362fdb-4919-1b44-9a4d-49ecfdea0035"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"2", ",", "4"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"6", ",", "8"}], "}"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"2", ",", "3"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"4", ",", "5"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUU\
ID->"066a3d90-7d07-3043-819d-660376405976"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "This is why scalar multiplication needs no name of its own. A scalar is the \
0-array; multiplying an array by one is the same entry-by-entry \
multiplication as multiplying it by another array of its own shape, with the \
single entry spread over the whole shape rather than matched index for index. \
One operation, one rule, and 2 a is ArrayTimes[NumericalArray[2], a] written \
shorter."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"f2551ac1-d6d6-5341-b309-37caa6672a4a"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["ArrayTimes[2, u] === ArrayTimes[NumericalArray[2], u]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"52a4ddf8-bde9-fc43-916f-70eecee0b6b9"],

Cell[BoxData["True"], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"a63e8e4c-c036-ce4b-b46a-f8df45a34434"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Arrays of different dimensions cannot be combined this way, and are refused \
rather than padded or truncated:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e1e235db-1b33-b144-ac6f-a2bd9428317d"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Quiet @ ArrayAdd[{1, 2}, {1, 2, 3}]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"35507f62-f5a7-f848-8785-f5e83b6c26a2"],

Cell[BoxData["$Failed"], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"0e1aea59-aa64-e243-a7e5-5a6257055733"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "ArrayMultiply refuses to mix numerical and symbolic arrays, because a \
contraction of the two would be neither and its entries would have no \
consistent reading. The entry-by-entry operations take one entry at a time, \
where a number meeting an expression has the obvious meaning, so there they \
may be combined; the result is a NumericalArray when every argument is \
numerical, and the plain entries otherwise."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"a665072d-1736-1740-b1e6-6d6a6bd0dc42"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
{2 SymbolicArray[c, {2}], NumericalArray[{1, 2}] + SymbolicArray[c, {2}]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"812274ae-b621-434f-9b33-9fcdf4146fc2"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"2", " ", 
      RowBox[{"c", "[", "1", "]"}]}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"2", " ", 
      RowBox[{"c", "[", "2", "]"}]}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"1", "+", 
      RowBox[{"c", "[", "1", "]"}]}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"2", "+", 
      RowBox[{"c", "[", "2", "]"}]}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUU\
ID->"5f75683f-bc0e-1b48-917b-ea88e480c08d"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
A symmetry that every argument declares is carried over to the result, since \
an entry-by-entry operation treats each index tuple separately. Where the \
arguments disagree nothing is claimed:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"08e21186-011a-bd4c-9cb3-1352404d2124"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
{ArraySymmetry @ ArrayAdd[NumericalArray[{{1, 2}, {2, 3}}, \"Integer\", \
\"Symmetric\"],
                           NumericalArray[{{0, 1}, {1, 0}}, \"Integer\", \
\"Symmetric\"]],
  ArraySymmetry @ ArrayAdd[NumericalArray[{{1, 2}, {2, 3}}, \"Integer\", \
\"Symmetric\"],
                           NumericalArray[u]]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"274d5e2f-4aea-464d-bbd6-5f07497af7ae"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"\<\"Symmetric\"\>", ",", "\<\"Rigid\"\>"}], "}"}]], "Output",Expres\
sionUUID->"026e32c7-0491-6c4b-b5fd-c6d653fa8b3c"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "The arguments are given directly rather than in a list, unlike \
ArrayMultiply, which takes a list of arrays and a specification alongside it. \
A list of arrays is itself an array, so ArrayAdd[{v1, v2}] would be \
ambiguous: it could be the sum of two vectors or the matrix they form. Given \
directly there is nothing to guess."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"ca949c03-4aba-d547-adb0-3eb87caa9204"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["8. Searching for equational identities", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"efc0e618-fb8d-6347-a41a-955329b9efe9"],

Cell["\<\
FindArrayEquations takes a list of array operations and a list of generic \
arguments, forms every way of nesting the operations over those arguments, \
and returns the equations that hold between them. Operations are ordinary \
functions of arrays, so they are easily built out of ArrayMultiply:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"db203585-5190-0e46-925c-983122b2da42"],

Cell["\<\
dot[u_List, w_List] := Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{u, w}, \"ij,jk->ik\"];
Fish[u_List, w_List, t_List] := Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{u, w, t}, \
\"ijp,qrp,qrk->ijk\"];
BM[u_List, w_List, t_List] := Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{u, w, t}, \
\"ijp,ipk,pjk->ijk\"];\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"f606cfde-0e6c-554d-8f7a-bf8007a89dfa"],

Cell["\<\
Ordinary matrix multiplication on three generic arguments finds \
associativity, and nothing else:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"55a3b510-9d89-0441-8cd4-d06d95ca55e4"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
FindArrayEquations[{dot}, {a1, a2, a3}, \"Order\" -> 2, \"Dimension\" -> 3]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"2aa5b696-f159-5540-982d-41c003c05f21"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
    RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a3"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
   RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], ",", "a3"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",\
ExpressionUUID->"825d3812-bb58-ae4a-9445-bc0bb9b3db86"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The Fish product on five generic arguments finds a twisted form of ternary \
associativity. One of the two equations below is plain associativity in the \
outer arguments; the other reverses the inner three:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7720ca36-c0c4-1544-8247-73f4fa3a5150"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["FindArrayEquations[{Fish}, {a1, a2, a3, a4, a5}]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"516d80b3-d392-ce45-88c6-3451c0967f64"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2", ",", 
      RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
       RowBox[{"a3", ",", "a4", ",", "a5"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
      RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
       RowBox[{"a4", ",", "a3", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], ",", "a5"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2", ",", 
      RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
       RowBox[{"a3", ",", "a4", ",", "a5"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
       RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2", ",", "a3"}], "]"}], ",", "a4", ",", "a5"}], 
     "]"}]}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"cee5668b-62ed-d74a-b9e0-\
5b50b4573193"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The Bhattacharya-Mesner product, on the same five arguments, satisfies \
nothing at all:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5f1ea0fc-ffb8-1447-ad6f-9b8735156220"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["FindArrayEquations[{BM}, {a1, a2, a3, a4, a5}]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"5f72de45-a742-574c-86a7-5b330c050b71"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"09345d89-8e74-354e-ba41-7688c95364e5"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "The search is in two stages, and that is what makes it quick. Every term is \
first evaluated on random integer arrays and only pairs that agree there are \
carried forward. Two terms equal as identities agree on any arrays, so \
nothing correct is ever thrown away; the numerical stage only proposes, and \
the symbolic stage disposes. The surviving candidates are then checked on \
symbolic arrays by expanding the difference of the two sides, which is exact \
for polynomials and far quicker than a general simplification."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"50aba022-326f-8f45-832d-c4c4e145ac02"],

Cell["\<\
Whether the arguments may be permuted is an option. With a fixed order only \
the nesting varies, which for matrix multiplication makes no difference, \
since associativity does not move its arguments:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"bcb627d8-05e2-3044-bbb9-ae19b32970cc"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
FindArrayEquations[{dot}, {a1, a2, a3}, \"Order\" -> 2, \"Dimension\" -> 3,
                    \"Permute\" -> False]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"fc2db1b8-dc78-d04c-b1b0-d5f173452e7b"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
    RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a3"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
   RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], ",", "a3"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",\
ExpressionUUID->"bd0ab597-b1c4-0d48-a9bd-18a76d4f008a"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "An identity verified on symbolic arrays of a given dimension is proved at \
that dimension, not at every dimension. The default is 2, which is small and \
quick; raising \"Dimension\" checks more widely. The Fish equations above \
come out the same at dimension 3, which is reassuring without being a proof \
in general."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"b4effb78-59d7-844c-9d37-30f153496268"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["More than one operation at a time", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"52b946ad-04b0-4342-80c7-820b61fd57d6"],

Cell["\<\
Operations of different arities may be searched together. A transpose is \
unary and a matrix product binary; on two generic arguments they give the law \
that transposing a product reverses it, and the law that transposing twice \
does nothing:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"80fea747-19a8-6e49-960f-a966162e06cf"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
perp[u_List] := Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{u}, \"ij->ji\"];
FindArrayEquations[{perp, dot}, {a1, a2}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"68fbb01d-9c3f-bb4d-94d7-6a66063b1c06"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"a1", "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "]"}]}]}], "}"}]], "Output",Expression\
UUID->"82e404c9-7ae2-5647-9bb8-9375e4fac63d"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "An operation of arity one is the exception to the rule that a term uses \
exactly the given arguments. It takes a term to a term of the same shape, so \
it may be applied over and over at any point without consuming an argument, \
and every term is offered wrapped in up to \"UnaryDepth\" nested unary \
operations, two by default. That is what brings the involutivity within \
reach: it needs the transpose twice over on one argument, where the \
anti-homomorphism law uses it once on each side."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"93899bf7-30ac-f24a-8437-6e0e8f822031"],

Cell["\<\
Setting the depth to one puts the involutivity back out of reach \[LongDash] \
and with it gone, the remaining law is reported in each of its forms instead \
of once, since without perp[perp[a]] == a there is nothing to rewrite one \
form into another:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ccaae127-7323-a942-894c-a97c6e8b8585"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["FindArrayEquations[{perp, dot}, {a1, a2}, \"UnaryDepth\" -> 1]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"c34260cf-c6f4-4d48-9ad3-1b171d0afeb1"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", 
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a2", ",", 
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], ",", "a2"}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}], ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}], ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], ",", "a2"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]}]}], "}"}]], "Output",\
ExpressionUUID->"a2bd52f7-f4a6-d444-99ef-c2800223941f"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The anti-homomorphism law moves its arguments, so holding them in the order \
given leaves only the involutivity, which does not:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a62f39c5-f1c6-7147-bc2e-323c2d6bda19"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
FindArrayEquations[{perp, dot}, {a1, a2}, \"Permute\" -> False]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"dde79d5b-775a-f74c-8f04-ea9217671ef6"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"a1", "\[Equal]", 
   RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID\
->"b1a9c658-3831-1643-9dbf-c0eae40a5e2b"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "Freely nested unary operations bring a flood with them. Every consequence \
of those two laws is an equation between terms of the search, and each would \
be reported in its own right: a hundred and more of them here, all saying the \
same two things over again. So an equation that follows from the others is \
left out. Each equation is read as a schema, its arguments standing for \
arbitrary terms, and turned into a rewrite rule that fires only when it makes \
a term strictly smaller; any number of such rules is then a terminating \
rewriting system, and an equation whose two sides rewrite to a common form is \
a consequence of the ones already kept. \"Reduce\" -> False reports \
everything found."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"fa9edfa5-3b00-a542-bc28-ee54c47799b3"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Length @ FindArrayEquations[{perp, dot}, {a1, a2}, \"Reduce\" -> False]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"0349d806-7ff3-6a4f-a44b-7ce24f83f5cd"],

Cell[BoxData["115"], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"11de3447-2e15-404b-8533-9a6d6968effe"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "What survives is a basis, not a canonical set. Which of several equivalent \
forms of a law is kept depends on the order the equations are considered in \
\[LongDash] the anti-homomorphism law above is reported with the transposes \
gathered on whichever side the ordering favours \[LongDash] and the reduction \
is by rewriting alone, so a consequence that rewriting cannot reach is still \
reported. A genuinely minimal set of axioms is the business of Knuth-Bendix \
completion, which this is not."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"827ef8a2-756f-d344-b292-d5786eb2d875"],

Cell["\<\
A trace and a matrix product give the cyclic property of the trace:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"43ca85aa-6c23-d44f-8c13-abf063c52173"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["FindArrayEquations[{trace, dot}, {a1, a2}]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"a17f6e35-e581-014f-be9e-8de54e3ba5d1"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"trace", "[", 
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
   RowBox[{"trace", "[", 
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUI\
D->"e4c51bd1-c6d4-934e-8c69-9aa1d2e9113d"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "The trace returns a scalar, so a nesting that feeds it back into a product \
cannot evaluate. Such a term is dropped rather than compared, which is what \
lets an operation of that shape sit at the top of a term without having to be \
excluded by hand."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"c3f8f3f2-352b-bc41-a244-c133f0bbb4c6"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "Neither of those searches was told the shape of its test arrays. With \
\"Order\" -> Automatic, the default, the smallest order on which every \
operation actually evaluates is used, which is 2 for matrix operations and 3 \
for the ternary array products. Operations are shape-specific enough that \
this is a search for the shape they are defined on rather than a guess."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"e0f0a694-0f12-7a48-90e5-291ae65776b0"],

Cell["\<\
Forcing a shape the operations cannot take says so, rather than coming back \
empty:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2b9a08bc-2cbc-f141-aa66-2e29268327a3"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Quiet @ FindArrayEquations[{perp, dot}, {a1, a2}, \"Order\" -> 4]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"3412c41e-c6b2-f44d-a94c-e85d0cac3051"],

Cell[BoxData["$Failed"], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"02efcb20-c7d8-3e47-bdd5-27a6462bb9e6"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "An empty result and a result that could not be computed are different \
answers, and conflating them would be the worst kind of quiet failure: it \
would read as a proof that no identity exists. Whenever no nesting evaluates, \
FindArrayEquations::eval is issued and $Failed returned."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"73b06aa1-efe4-344e-a804-e45ec4a248ce"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["9. Scope and limits", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"2cd755bb-5c18-4042-a1a2-90cf6434bb45"],

Cell["\<\
A scalar is the array of order zero: no indices, no dimensions, one entry. \
That is what a full contraction leaves behind and what multiplies an array \
entry by entry, so the alternative \[LongDash] treating a plain value as \
something other than an array \[LongDash] would have needed a separate rule \
at each of those places.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b7997611-209c-3845-a584-359b6f1e98cd"],

Cell["\<\
Only the three symmetries named in the plan are recognized: rigid, cyclic and \
symmetric. Arbitrary index symmetry groups, and antisymmetry with its sign, \
are not implemented.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c58b0bbf-d28a-1348-8af0-31e1d32dc3f8"],

Cell["\<\
The symmetry of a numerical array is checked by testing invariance under a \
generating set of the group, one transposition and one rotation, rather than \
under every permutation. That is enough, and it keeps the check cheap for \
arrays of high order.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"689419f5-0dc7-1f4f-b4ed-5f3d2ce645c7"],

Cell["\<\
The file tests/HypermatrixExamples.wls runs the examples in this notebook as \
assertions, plus 1300 randomized checks: that order, dimensions and \
regularity always agree with the entries; that a symmetrized array is always \
accepted as symmetric and a cyclically symmetrized one as cyclic; that the \
canonical order of a hypermatrix never depends on the order its arrays were \
given in; and that the result really is sorted by the three stated criteria.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6a2a9015-f87e-7c4f-982a-9788514b7458"],

Cell["\<\
The file tests/ArrayAlgebraExamples.wls does the same for the special arrays, \
ArrayMultiply, the entry-by-entry operations and FindArrayEquations, checking \
the contraction against Dot, Tr, Diagonal, Transpose and Outer, and \
confirming that every equation reported really holds on arrays the search \
never saw.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"215dd2e9-3f23-aa40-86d4-d58939623c1d"],

Cell["Next: the graphical rendering of 1-, 2- and 3-arrays.", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"895c2832-2df3-8945-aca8-c428bcba3ec5"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]]
},
WindowSize->{1920, 1017.75},
WindowMargins->{{-6, Automatic}, {Automatic, -6}},
